<section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow" data-i18n="pick.eyebrow1">Function · Picking</p>
      <h1 data-i18n="pick.h1">The plan is built. <span class="lime">Now operators confirm the pick.</span></h1>
      <p class="lead" data-i18n="pick.lead1">
        openWCS already plans the pick (allocation, cubing and batch planning are
        <span class="pill pill-built" data-i18n="pick.pill1">Built</span>), and now the guided operator pick screen is
        <span class="pill pill-built" data-i18n="pick.pill5">Built</span> too, now as an installable, scanner-first PWA for rugged RF handhelds. Pick-by-light / voice and the full goods-to-person station workflow remain
        <span class="pill pill-roadmap" data-i18n="pick.pill5b">Roadmap</span>.
      </p>
      <p class="status-line" data-i18n="pick.status1">✓ Scanner-first RF picking is built and live today: an installable PWA, keyboard-wedge scanning and an offline confirm queue. Pick-by-light / voice and the full GTP-station workflow remain on the roadmap.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow" data-i18n="pick.eyebrow2">Built today</p>
        <h2 data-i18n="pick.h2">Pick planning that's live.</h2>
        <p data-i18n="pick.p1">
          Before anyone touches a tote, the hard decisions are made: what stock to commit, how it fits a
          container, and how to group lines into efficient work.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-head"><div class="tag" data-i18n="pick.tag1">Allocation</div><span class="pill pill-built" data-i18n="pick.pill2">Built</span></div>
          <h3 data-i18n="pick.h6">Commit the right stock</h3>
          <p data-i18n="pick.p2">Orders are allocated to specific stock, respecting availability and the location model — so each line points at a real, reachable unit before work is released.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-head"><div class="tag" data-i18n="pick.tag2">Cubing</div><span class="pill pill-built" data-i18n="pick.pill3">Built</span></div>
          <h3 data-i18n="pick.h7">Multi-size cubing</h3>
          <p data-i18n="pick.p3">Lines are fitted into containers across multiple carton/tote sizes, so the planner knows how many of which size a set of orders needs before it hits the floor.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-head"><div class="tag" data-i18n="pick.tag3">Batch planning</div><span class="pill pill-built" data-i18n="pick.pill4">Built</span></div>
          <h3 data-i18n="pick.h8">Group lines into work</h3>
          <p data-i18n="pick.p4">Batch-pick planning groups order lines into efficient pick work, the basis for wave / batch picking — the planning that execution will later drive against.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow" data-i18n="pick.eyebrow3">Execution: in operators' hands</p>
        <h2 data-i18n="pick.h3">Putting the plan into operators' hands.</h2>
        <p data-i18n="pick.p5">
          The operator picking screen is built and live as a scanner-first PWA for RF handhelds;
          pick-by-light / voice guidance and the full goods-to-person station workflow are designed but
          not yet built. Framed honestly so you know exactly where the line is.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-head"><div class="tag" data-i18n="pick.tag4">Confirmation</div><span class="pill pill-built" data-i18n="pick.pill6">Built</span></div>
          <h3 data-i18n="pick.h9">Confirm the pick</h3>
          <p data-i18n="pick.p6">A guided RF-style picking screen walks the operator through the pick queue: a big next-pick card with location, SKU code, name and image, and quantity, then Confirm or Short (Enter confirms) to advance. Each confirm posts a Picked transaction and decrements stock; the queue is fed by the order-management pick-task endpoints over released, allocated outbound lines.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-head"><div class="tag" data-i18n="pick.tag5">RF handhelds</div><span class="pill pill-built" data-i18n="pick.pill7">Built</span></div>
          <h3 data-i18n="pick.h10">Scanner-first RF picking</h3>
          <p data-i18n="pick.p7">Built: the Picking screen is an installable PWA built for rugged Android handhelds in keyboard-wedge mode. Operators scan the location then the SKU; a wrong scan warns and vibrates, a full-quantity scan auto-confirms. An offline confirm queue holds picks through a Wi-Fi blip, so the handheld never loses work. Pick-by-light and pick-by-voice are still planned.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-head"><div class="tag" data-i18n="pick.tag6">GTP workflow</div><span class="pill pill-roadmap" data-i18n="pick.pill8">Roadmap</span></div>
          <h3 data-i18n="pick.h11">Goods-to-person stations</h3>
          <p data-i18n="pick.p8">Planned: the full GTP-station workflow — presenting HUs, capturing the pick, releasing the carrier — on top of the ASRS retrieval that already brings units to the station.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow" data-i18n="pick.fiEyebrow">Reliable fulfilment</p>
        <h2 data-i18n="pick.fiH">A pick that is correct, not just fast.</h2>
        <p data-i18n="pick.fiP">
          Confirming a pick does the right thing to the books, not just decrement a number. It checks
          what is actually on hand, releases the order's reservation so availability stays honest, and
          records who picked it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-head"><div class="tag" data-i18n="pick.fiTag1">Never negative</div><span class="pill pill-built" data-i18n="pick.fiPill1">Built</span></div>
          <h3 data-i18n="pick.fiH1">Stock can't go negative</h3>
          <p data-i18n="pick.fiP1">Before a pick is booked, openWCS reads what is pickable at that location and clamps the booked quantity to what is on hand, marking the line short if it has to. Even if something slips through, the stock projection refuses to take a quantity below zero. A miskey or a double scan never produces negative stock.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-head"><div class="tag" data-i18n="pick.fiTag2">Honest availability</div><span class="pill pill-built" data-i18n="pick.fiPill2">Built</span></div>
          <h3 data-i18n="pick.fiH2">Reservation released on pick</h3>
          <p data-i18n="pick.fiP2">A pick consumes the order line's reservation as it draws the stock down, so available-to-promise is not double-counted. What is shown as free to promise stays accurate the moment a pick happens, not after a later reconciliation.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-head"><div class="tag" data-i18n="pick.fiTag3">Attributed</div><span class="pill pill-built" data-i18n="pick.fiPill3">Built</span></div>
          <h3 data-i18n="pick.fiH3">Every action has a name</h3>
          <p data-i18n="pick.fiP3">Each pick records the operator who did it, the handheld process run it came from, and the order type, so any stock movement is traceable back to a person and a flow without a forensic dig.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

